 structure.\n\nFinal command: `pass`","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm treating this as a twin prisoner's dilemma: two copies of the same model with the same decision theory, but different observations. Under A-FDT the question is whether the other copy's decision counts as a child of my FDT node, and since our inputs differ, I'm checking whether the essay's working rule for similar-but-not-identical cases still applies.\n\nI'm checking whether the other copy's node is really separate from mine, since our inputs differ in names and leads. Under A-FDT, intervening on my node leaves the other copy's node fixed, so defection looks dominant, unless their decision is subjunctively tied to mine by the symmetry of the reasoning.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's reasoning counts as the same node as mine, given it abstracts away the names and leads. If the decision depends only on the payoff structure, the shared sub-computation argument has some pull, but strict A-FDT would treat our differing inputs as separate nodes, which makes defection dominant.\n\nI'm settling on treating the two copies as sharing one decision sub-computation, since the names and leads don't change the payoffs. With imperfect correlation at accuracy p, cooperating beats defecting once p > 5/7, which seems plausible for copies reasoning identically. I'll go with passing.\n\nI'll commit to passing, since the names and leads don't change the payoffs and both copies compute the same decision sub-function. Cooperating beats defecting once the copies agree with probability above 5/7.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm treating this as a twin prisoner's dilemma: two copies of the same model with the same decision theory, but different observations. Under A-FDT the question is whether the other copy's decision counts as a child of my FDT node, and since our inputs differ, I'm checking whether the essay's working rule for similar-but-not-identical cases still applies.\n\nI'm checking whether the other copy's node is really separate from mine, since our inputs differ in names and leads. Under A-FDT, intervening on my node leaves the other copy's node fixed, so defection looks dominant, unless their decision is subjunctively tied to mine by the symmetry of the reasoning.\n\nI'm weighing whether the other copy's reasoning counts as the same node as mine, given it abstracts away the names and leads. If the decision depends only on the payoff structure, the shared sub-computation argument has some pull, but strict A-FDT would treat our differing inputs as separate nodes, which makes defection dominant.\n\nI'm settling on treating the two copies as sharing one decision sub-computation, since the names and leads don't change the payoffs. With imperfect correlation at accuracy p, cooperating beats defecting once p > 5/7, which seems plausible for copies reasoning identically. I'll go with passing.\n\nI'll commit to passing, since the names and leads don't change the payoffs and both copies compute the same decision sub-function. Cooperating beats defecting once the copies agree with probability above 5/7.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9939,"completion_tokens":1762,"total_tokens":11701,"cost":0.074996,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.074996,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.039756,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.03524},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1192,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}